1
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,110
I was unstoppable.

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:06,380
I mean, I was... I was on fire.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:09,840
It was like my mind and my body were totally connected,

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,400
like-like athletes must feel when they're in the zone.

5
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:17,170
Again, it was miniature golf.

6
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:20,240
Admit it, you're a little turned on.

7
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:22,030
You can't be this proud.

8
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:22,850
Why not?

9
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:25,890
Because I beat you.

10
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:30,560
Oh, good. You're back.

11
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,940
We have some exciting news.

12
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:36,090
Okay.

13
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:40,060
As you know, Amy and I have been together a long time,

14
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:43,960
and a lot of things I never thought possible

15
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:46,530
now seem possible.

16
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:48,830
Okay...

17
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:52,190
After a careful evaluation of our relationship,

18
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:54,640
we decided that the time was right to

19
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:57,330
take a step forward.

20
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,440
Okay.

21
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:07,690
Do you want to say it?

22
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:09,880
Let's say it together.

23
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,560
We're getting a turtle!

24
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:19,990
This is why I've been saying we should keep champagne on ice.

25
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:22,000
- Sarcasm? - Yes.

26
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:22,710
Okay.

27
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:24,680
That was tricky, 'cause when it comes to alcohol,

28
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:27,470
she generally means business.

29
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:30,360
- Well, we're-we're very happy for you. - Yes.

30
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:31,380
Thank you.

31
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:34,430
Acquiring a joint pet is a big step for us.

32
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:35,640
No. It's true.

33
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:37,840
It means that we care so much about each other,

34
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:41,630
there's enough left over for an eight-ounce reptile.

35
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:43,260
Why a turtle?

36
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:46,570
After much deliberation, we've determined that a turtle

37
00:01:46,570 --> 00:01:48,180
is the ideal pet.

38
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:51,240
They don't shed fur, they don't make noise...

39
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,130
For Halloween, we can dress him as a cobblestone.

40
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,050
And if he ever goes berserk, I know I can outrun him.

41
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:02,540
Coincidentally, that's also why I chose you as a roommate.

42
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:05,020
Well, congratulations.

43
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:06,300
Who would've thought you two would be the first

44
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,730
in our group to start a family?

45
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:11,360
That's what I said!

46
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:40,150
So, where's your roommate tonight?

47
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:42,000
Well, I thought it was a little unfair

48
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,160
that she's always here,

49
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:45,370
and you never get a chance to stay over, so...

50
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:47,280
I killed her.

51
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:50,870
But remember our agreement?

52
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:52,590
You can joke about murdering people,

53
00:02:52,590 --> 00:02:56,110
but you have to say, "just kidding."

54
00:02:56,110 --> 00:02:58,110
And the more important thing to remember is

55
00:02:58,110 --> 00:03:00,600
that I'd never hurt you.

56
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:08,580
Oh, crap.

57
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,370
I have to run over to the hospital and check on a patient.

58
00:03:11,370 --> 00:03:13,130
That's okay. I can come back later.

59
00:03:13,130 --> 00:03:14,970
No. No, don't be silly.

60
00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:17,230
I won't be gone long. Just stay here.

61
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:18,650
Okay, cool.

62
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:20,480
Oh, and you're sure your roommate's not gonna come back

63
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:21,480
while I'm here alone, right?

64
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:22,910
'Cause that'll be awkward.

65
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:24,590
Oh, don't worry. She's in Palm Springs.

66
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:25,630
Oh, good.

67
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:28,060
Well, her torso is.

68
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,430
Just kidding.

69
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,340
I put her in a wood chipper.

70
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:39,860
Oh, dear Lord. Oh, dear Lord.

71
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:41,100
It's okay.

72
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,900
You made it. We're fine.

73
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,460
That was a lot of puppies.

74
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:49,520
Let's forget about them, and...

75
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:51,780
pick ourselves out a nice turtle.

76
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,900
Oh, how about this one up on the log?

77
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:56,400
Oh, I don't know.

78
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:58,990
He kind of looks like a jerk.

79
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:02,180
How about this one?

80
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:04,580
Well, he's barely moving. He looks half dead.

81
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:07,580
I know. I like him, too.

82
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:11,460
Hi, little guy.

83
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,780
How'd you like to come home with us?

84
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:15,470
You'll be living with me

85
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:17,830
because we don't live in the same house.

86
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:19,200
But that's not your fault.

87
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,210
Like you, we're taking it ridiculously slow.

88
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,890
You'll stay with me when he's at Comic-Con or away for work.

89
00:04:28,890 --> 00:04:33,730
Or if they accept Daddy's application to live on Mars.

90
00:04:34,390 --> 00:04:36,360
What are you talking about?

91
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,640
Oh, there's this company

92
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:40,410
that's attempting to establish a colony on Mars,

93
00:04:40,410 --> 00:04:42,690
and I applied to be among the first to go.

94
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:44,620
What should we name him?

95
00:04:44,620 --> 00:04:48,180
You know, I came in thinking "Seth," but...

96
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:51,810
he kind of looks Italian.

97
00:04:52,190 --> 00:04:54,020
You applied for a mission

98
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:55,930
to be a colonist on another planet,

99
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:58,650
and couldn't be bothered to tell me?

100
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:01,590
Would you have approved?

101
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:03,210
Of course not.

102
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:05,310
Well, based on your reaction,

103
00:05:05,310 --> 00:05:08,090
it looks like I made the right choice.

104
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:11,430
Isn't that right, Giuseppe?

105
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:19,320
Listen, I-I have a little confession to make.

106
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,900
Aw, is this your first time?

107
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:27,480
No, I got you something for Valentine's Day,

108
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:29,360
and I was too embarrassed to give it to you.

109
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,740
Well, why?

110
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:35,050
Well, 'cause I got it at the dirty store.

111
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,470
You went to the dirty store without me?!

112
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:42,710
In sunglasses and a hat after I parked two blocks away.

113
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:44,340
Well, get it! Get it for me!

114
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:45,420
- Yeah? - Yeah.

115
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:47,710
Okay, uh, well, I hope it's fun.

116
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:49,690
I mean, it-it comes with paints, and-and

117
00:05:49,690 --> 00:05:52,130
it's kind of creative and artistic.

118
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:55,670
Okay, did you go to the dirty store or Michaels?

119
00:05:57,490 --> 00:05:58,150
No, no.

120
00:05:58,150 --> 00:06:00,130
We cover ourselves in body paint,

121
00:06:00,130 --> 00:06:04,480
and then-then we get on this big canvas and do our thing.

122
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:06,250
Whoa, that's kind of a big step for a guy

123
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:09,870
who only recently agreed to take his socks off.

124
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:11,580
You're making fun of me. Forget it.

125
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:13,180
No! No, come on, I want to do it.

126
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:14,540
- Really? - Yeah,

127
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:15,710
let's you, me,

128
00:06:15,710 --> 00:06:19,090
and your very, very pale feet make some art.

129
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:21,670
Hey, when you got back to this store, I want to go with you.

130
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:23,080
Okay, but it's a drive--

131
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:25,390
the one I went to is in San Diego.

132
00:06:28,150 --> 00:06:30,110
How are the taxes going?

133
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:31,420
Okay.

134
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:35,370
But you got a lot of receipts for the Lego store in here.

135
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:37,830
Those are business expenses.

136
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,900
You can write those off.

137
00:06:40,690 --> 00:06:44,660
A $200 R2-D2 is a business expense?

138
00:06:45,300 --> 00:06:46,300
Oh, Bernie,

139
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:48,090
you're gonna have to sound a lot more confident

140
00:06:48,090 --> 00:06:50,110
when we get audited.

141
00:06:54,990 --> 00:06:56,760
Hey, what's up?

142
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:58,380
Eh, Emily ran back to the hospital,

143
00:06:58,380 --> 00:07:00,610
so I'm just hanging out at her place.

144
00:07:00,610 --> 00:07:02,340
What are you, uh, what are you doing?

145
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:06,280
Just playing video games while Bernie does the taxes.

146
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:08,280
What are you, a little kid?

147
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:11,890
Is she gonna cut your dinner into little pieces, too?

148
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:16,610
She doesn't have to-- I filled up on jelly beans.

149
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:19,210
So, uh, what game are you...

150
00:07:20,610 --> 00:07:22,640
Crap.

151
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:23,660
What's wrong?

152
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:25,840
I can't get Emily's nightstand to close.

153
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:26,820
So?

154
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:28,850
She's gonna know I was looking in it.

155
00:07:28,850 --> 00:07:31,000
Why were you looking in it?

156
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:31,750
Well, there's a question

157
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:34,480
I better have a good answer to before she gets back.

158
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:36,300
Okay, calm down.

159
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:37,800
There's probably something jammed behind it.

160
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,080
Just, uh, pull it out and see what's there.

161
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,090
Hang on.

162
00:07:45,350 --> 00:07:47,540
Oh... no. Oh, no.

163
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:49,710
Oh, God, no!

164
00:07:50,330 --> 00:07:53,110
You know what, you sound busy-- I'm gonna let you go.

165
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:55,660
Dude, the whole front came off.

166
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:57,610
Now she's gonna know I was snooping.

167
00:07:57,610 --> 00:07:58,760
What's happening?

168
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:02,310
Raj was snooping through Emily's drawers and broke one.

169
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:05,030
I'm gonna miss her.

170
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:10,020
So what do you think?

171
00:08:10,820 --> 00:08:12,920
I thought it'd be a little more...

172
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:15,890
just more.

173
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:22,290
I'm not even sure why we were out of breath.

174
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:26,830
Uh, I mean, d-did we move at all?

175
00:08:27,170 --> 00:08:29,170
Maybe along the z-axis,

176
00:08:29,170 --> 00:08:32,440
but x and y are looking pretty sad.

177
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:35,160
Okay, come on. We are not old boring people.

178
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:36,940
We can do better than this.

179
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:37,610
Uh, th-that's true.

180
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:39,820
How late did we stay up last night?

181
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:42,220
Almost 1:00 a.M.

182
00:08:42,670 --> 00:08:45,790
Damn straight, almost 1:00 a.M.

183
00:08:46,850 --> 00:08:48,470
And we weren't even watching TV.

184
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:51,710
We were watching Netflix, like the kids do.

185
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:53,860
Yeah, is it a comedy? Is it a drama?

186
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:55,700
Nobody knows.

187
00:08:57,150 --> 00:08:58,790
Now, come on. We are gonna do this.

188
00:08:58,790 --> 00:08:59,490
Yeah. You get the paint,

189
00:08:59,490 --> 00:09:00,910
I'll rest for 30-40 minutes,

190
00:09:00,910 --> 00:09:03,160
and then we do this!

191
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:06,780
I can't believe you almost had me bring

192
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:09,420
a wild animal into my home.

193
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,950
No one told you to poke the turtle's face.

194
00:09:13,730 --> 00:09:16,720
I was playing "Got Your Nose."

195
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:20,500
That's how you get children to like you.

196
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:23,380
I'm surprised you even care if he likes you,

197
00:09:23,380 --> 00:09:26,370
since you're planning on leaving the planet the first chance you get.

198
00:09:26,370 --> 00:09:27,390
Ugh, this again.

199
00:09:27,390 --> 00:09:31,000
Amy, I've already had one new hole torn in my body today.

200
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,700
I don't need another one.

201
00:09:33,770 --> 00:09:35,800
Sheldon, I know the odds of you

202
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:37,920
even going to Mars are incredibly small,

203
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,040
but it still hurts that you would volunteer for something

204
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:44,130
that would take you away from me forever.

205
00:09:44,130 --> 00:09:45,900
So you're saying you wouldn't leave me

206
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:47,780
for the chance to be one of the first humans

207
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:49,780
to colonize another planet?

208
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:51,440
I would at least mention it

209
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,650
before filling out the application.

210
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:56,560
Huh, that's exactly what Leonard, Wolowitz, Koothrappali,

211
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:59,100
Bernadette and Penny said.

212
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:01,480
And who says you could even survive

213
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:03,110
an interplanetary mission, anyway?

214
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:05,530
You could barely survive a tiny turtle bite.

215
00:10:05,920 --> 00:10:07,630
First of all, this...

216
00:10:07,630 --> 00:10:09,800
has only made me stronger.

217
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:12,160
But beyond that, all I did

218
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:14,330
was fill out an application.

219
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:15,690
You know what?

220
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:18,210
Go to Mars, Sheldon.

221
00:10:21,070 --> 00:10:23,450
Is there anything I can do to cheer you up?

222
00:10:23,450 --> 00:10:25,060
No. Forget it.

223
00:10:25,750 --> 00:10:28,350
What if I play the Star Trek theme on my nose?

224
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:29,940
Please don't.

225
00:10:37,330 --> 00:10:39,290
Yep, you're mad.

226
00:10:44,510 --> 00:10:46,670
Okay, let me see the damage.

227
00:10:46,670 --> 00:10:47,930
Hang on.

228
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:50,760
Well, I think you broke the dowels.

229
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:52,140
You're not gonna have time to glue it back on--

230
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:53,580
you'll have to nail it.

231
00:10:53,580 --> 00:10:54,470
With what?

232
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:57,740
Does she have any pillows or wine glasses?

233
00:10:57,740 --> 00:10:58,920
She does.

234
00:10:58,920 --> 00:10:59,640
Great.

235
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:02,770
Neither of those. Try a hammer!

236
00:11:05,190 --> 00:11:06,310
Did that feel good?

237
00:11:06,310 --> 00:11:08,720
You feel like a big man now?

238
00:11:12,190 --> 00:11:14,900
I was going to make you red zinger,

239
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:17,510
but since Mars is the red planet,

240
00:11:17,510 --> 00:11:20,010
I went with peppermint.

241
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,910
Why do you even want to do this?

242
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:27,370
Actually...

243
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:29,540
as part of the application,

244
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:32,570
I was required to make a short video

245
00:11:32,570 --> 00:11:35,260
answering that very question.

246
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:36,290
You want to see it?

247
00:11:36,290 --> 00:11:37,970
Can't you just tell me?

248
00:11:37,970 --> 00:11:40,020
But I made a video.

249
00:11:42,770 --> 00:11:44,530
I'm Dr. Sheldon Cooper,

250
00:11:44,530 --> 00:11:47,490
and I'd like to tell you why I should be chosen to...

251
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:51,550
go to Mars!

252
00:11:52,770 --> 00:11:54,210
I'm exceedingly smart.

253
00:11:54,210 --> 00:11:56,270
I graduated college at 14.

254
00:11:56,270 --> 00:11:58,250
While my brother was getting an STD

255
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:01,000
I was getting a PhD.

256
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:06,190
Penicillin can't take this away.

257
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,280
Being in close quarters, cleanliness is important

258
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:13,450
and... my hygiene is impeccable.

259
00:12:13,450 --> 00:12:15,660
In fact, animals don't trust me

260
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:17,810
because I smell like nothing.

261
00:12:17,810 --> 00:12:18,780
Yeah...

262
00:12:19,730 --> 00:12:22,260
Literally nothing.

263
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:26,090
During the seven-month space flight,

264
00:12:26,090 --> 00:12:29,870
I can keep up morale with my wacky sense of humor.

265
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:33,440
Hey, Leonard...

266
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:36,760
is there any peanut brittle left in that can?

267
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:39,670
You mean this weirdly suspicious one?

268
00:12:39,670 --> 00:12:42,400
Yes. Open it and check.

269
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:48,960
I don't get it.

270
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:50,390
There's actually peanut brittle in...

271
00:12:57,610 --> 00:13:00,180
Please go to Mars.

272
00:13:01,540 --> 00:13:03,270
But on a more serious note:

273
00:13:03,270 --> 00:13:05,760
The most important reason I want to go to Mars is that

274
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:08,520
I believe, as a scientist,

275
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:13,160
it's my duty to push the boundaries of human knowledge forward.

276
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:16,350
Now, I know that life on Mars will be difficult.

277
00:13:16,350 --> 00:13:19,110
But life here on Earth is no picnic.

278
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:22,180
Also, picnics are no picnic.

279
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:24,220
Where should we go for lunch?

280
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:26,710
Oh, I know, the ground.

281
00:13:27,710 --> 00:13:28,730
In conclusion,

282
00:13:28,730 --> 00:13:30,360
thank you for considering me

283
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,810
for this journey of a lifetime.

284
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:37,120
To Mars!

285
00:13:39,450 --> 00:13:41,690
Afterwards, Leonard blew his nose,

286
00:13:41,690 --> 00:13:44,230
and pie came out.

287
00:13:47,830 --> 00:13:50,020
Right there, right there,

288
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:51,840
Oh, God, that's it!

289
00:13:54,730 --> 00:13:56,720
How did you get paint in your eye?

290
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:59,720
Because you wouldn't let me wear safety goggles!

291
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:05,880
This looks terrible.

292
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:07,090
She-she's gonna know.

293
00:14:07,090 --> 00:14:07,900
It's fine.

294
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:09,660
You just need two more nails.

295
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:10,600
Okay, where should I put them?

296
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:12,790
In Emily's eyes.

297
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:16,630
You're not helping.

298
00:14:16,630 --> 00:14:18,730
Well, maybe this is what you get for snooping.

299
00:14:18,730 --> 00:14:20,810
You know, it-it's bad enough that I have to deal with this...

300
00:14:20,810 --> 00:14:22,530
Raj? I'm back.

301
00:14:22,530 --> 00:14:24,480
Oh, no.

302
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:30,140
How was the hospital?

303
00:14:30,140 --> 00:14:31,670
Fine.

304
00:14:31,670 --> 00:14:33,630
What were you doing in there?

305
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:37,250
Uh... w-well...

306
00:14:37,630 --> 00:14:39,200
Okay, look, I-I don't want to lie to you.

307
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:41,680
I-I got curious, I was looking around...

308
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:44,640
and I broke the drawer on your night stand.

309
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:46,560
You were looking in my night stand?

310
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:47,660
Yeah.

311
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:49,540
So, the first time I leave you alone,

312
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:51,380
you snoop on me?

313
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:54,140
You've never snooped around my apartment?

314
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:55,450
No!

315
00:14:55,770 --> 00:14:57,170
Come on, think back.

316
00:14:57,170 --> 00:14:59,540
It would really help if you had!

317
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:02,190
I can't believe you don't trust me!

318
00:15:02,190 --> 00:15:04,040
She sounds really mad.

319
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:06,970
We should hang up.

320
00:15:06,970 --> 00:15:09,030
Yeah, we should.

321
00:15:09,030 --> 00:15:10,540
But we're not going to, are we?

322
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:12,670
Not a chance.

323
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:15,770
What happened to "snooping is wrong"?

324
00:15:15,770 --> 00:15:17,570
Howard, you're going to jail for tax fraud.

325
00:15:17,570 --> 00:15:19,090
Who cares?

326
00:15:21,870 --> 00:15:23,280
Well, we did it.

327
00:15:24,330 --> 00:15:25,950
We sure did.

328
00:15:25,950 --> 00:15:27,840
I mean, I was on fire!

329
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:30,310
I-I was in the zone, like an athlete.

330
00:15:30,310 --> 00:15:33,160
Sweetie, I beat you at this, too.

331
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:35,180
So, where do we want to hang it?

332
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:36,330
What? Are you kidding?

333
00:15:36,330 --> 00:15:37,730
We're not hanging it!

334
00:15:37,730 --> 00:15:39,640
But it's an expression of our love.

335
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:42,820
And our butts! Not hanging it!

336
00:15:43,570 --> 00:15:45,970
Seems a shame to throw it away.

337
00:15:45,970 --> 00:15:47,220
Yeah.

338
00:15:48,670 --> 00:15:49,570
We could give it to Sheldon,

339
00:15:49,570 --> 00:15:52,050
and tell him William Shatner painted it.

340
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:57,600
God, I love you. I love you so much.

341
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:02,260
I think I'm gonna go home.

342
00:16:03,450 --> 00:16:05,160
Why?

343
00:16:05,860 --> 00:16:08,690
I really don't understand what's happening here.

344
00:16:08,690 --> 00:16:11,460
You know, Sheldon, at any other time,

345
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:13,690
learning that you had plans to go live on Mars

346
00:16:13,690 --> 00:16:15,800
would be a slow news day.

347
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:17,280
But a couple of hours ago,

348
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:19,230
we were getting a turtle.

349
00:16:19,670 --> 00:16:21,720
And silly as it sounds,

350
00:16:22,350 --> 00:16:24,560
I thought that meant something.

351
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:27,320
Amy!

352
00:16:28,410 --> 00:16:31,170
Oh, why didn't I give her Sleepy Time tea?

353
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:34,820
Amy, wait!

354
00:16:34,820 --> 00:16:37,860
Getting a turtle meant a great deal to me, too.

355
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:38,740
Sure.

356
00:16:38,740 --> 00:16:41,180
Unless something better comes along.

357
00:16:41,720 --> 00:16:45,210
Do you want me to withdraw my application?

358
00:16:45,710 --> 00:16:47,320
What I want is for us

359
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:50,620
to be planning our future together.

360
00:16:51,900 --> 00:16:54,690
And in that future,

361
00:16:54,690 --> 00:16:57,460
are we on the same planet?

362
00:16:57,460 --> 00:17:00,630
Yeah, because I've seen people make the long-distance thing work.

363
00:17:00,630 --> 00:17:01,830
We're on the same planet!

364
00:17:01,830 --> 00:17:02,830
Okay!

365
00:17:06,540 --> 00:17:09,370
Does that planet have to be Earth?

366
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:12,540
Are you asking me to go to Mars with you?

367
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:14,210
I am.

368
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:18,100
Yeah, if I'm going to a barren, lifeless environment,

369
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:22,390
where the chances of survival are slim to none,

370
00:17:22,390 --> 00:17:24,960
I want you there with me.

371
00:17:29,130 --> 00:17:30,500
Why don't we go back to the apartment,

372
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:32,580
and fill out your application?

373
00:17:33,470 --> 00:17:35,290
Okay.

374
00:17:36,570 --> 00:17:38,720
I suppose being the first people

375
00:17:38,720 --> 00:17:41,300
on a new planet would be incredibly exciting.

376
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:42,950
Oh, I know.

377
00:17:42,950 --> 00:17:46,520
We could be the first to plant a flag on Mars.

378
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:50,100
We could be the first to watch Mars Attacks! On Mars.

379
00:17:50,930 --> 00:17:52,230
We could be the first to say

380
00:17:52,230 --> 00:17:56,180
"Good Lord, what on Mars are you talking about?"

381
00:17:56,900 --> 00:17:57,750
You know,

382
00:17:58,380 --> 00:18:00,170
we could also be the first people

383
00:18:00,170 --> 00:18:02,450
to procreate on Mars.

384
00:18:03,930 --> 00:18:07,070
You just can't keep it in your space pants, can you?

385
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:10,040
Think about it.

386
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:11,210
If we had a family there,

387
00:18:11,210 --> 00:18:13,390
our kids would be Martians.

388
00:18:15,530 --> 00:18:17,900
They would, wouldn't they?

389
00:18:19,210 --> 00:18:21,740
We could give them cool Martian names.

390
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:24,130
And we could teach them about Martian history.

391
00:18:24,130 --> 00:18:26,130
Like, who planted those flags?

392
00:18:26,130 --> 00:18:29,390
And, uh, where did that copy of Mars Attacks! Come from?

393
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:34,070
I guess we'll have to make a new video together, as a couple.

394
00:18:34,070 --> 00:18:35,920
Good idea.

395
00:18:37,410 --> 00:18:39,730
And since you've had such a rough day,

396
00:18:39,730 --> 00:18:42,760
I'm gonna let you throw the pie in Leonard's face.

397
00:18:49,310 --> 00:18:50,820
Thank you for forgiving me.

398
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:52,330
It's okay.

399
00:18:52,330 --> 00:18:55,260
At some point, we were bound to have our first fight.

400
00:18:55,260 --> 00:18:57,020
Well, it almost happened when you called

401
00:18:57,020 --> 00:19:00,070
my apple pie crust "Doughy."

402
00:19:01,990 --> 00:19:03,020
The truth is you were right.

403
00:19:03,020 --> 00:19:05,600
And I was just angry at myself.

404
00:19:06,830 --> 00:19:08,740
Can I ask you one thing?

405
00:19:08,740 --> 00:19:10,460
Of course. What?

406
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:13,850
Did you look in my closet?

407
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:15,090
No. Just the drawer.

408
00:19:15,090 --> 00:19:16,960
You promise you didn't look in the closet?

409
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:18,450
I promise.

410
00:19:18,450 --> 00:19:19,470
Why, what's in there?

411
00:19:19,470 --> 00:19:20,870
Don't worry about it.

412
00:19:22,010 --> 00:19:23,330
Good night.

